            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Observation: Efficiency Anxiety in Proxy Account Opening</h2>
<p>This week, our customer service team received several typical inquiries: an operations lead for a utility app complained that they couldn't check account status and didn't know if the account was under review or rejected; a finance staff member from a cross-border e-commerce team reported that reconciling recharge records and spending reports manually took most of the day for just 10 accounts; another team asked why the agent claimed 5 accounts but only 3 showed in the backend.</p>
<p>These issues point to one core problem: proxy account opening services have long relied on manual follow-ups and fragmented communication, lacking unified visual management tools.</p>
<h2>Three High-Frequency Pain Points</h2>
<p><strong>Account Status Black Box</strong><br />
Proxy account opening involves multiple stages—submission, review, supplementary materials, account activation—and any stage can stall. If teams can only wait for WeChat replies from agents, information delays are inevitable. One game publishing team's media buyer shared that they once missed an account suspension notice, leaving recharged balance untraceable.</p>
<p><strong>Delayed Recharge Alerts</strong><br />
Insufficient ad account balance triggers automatic pause in ad delivery. For teams managing multiple accounts, manual monitoring inevitably has gaps. A typical case this week: an e-commerce team lost nearly 2 hours of delivery during peak promotion hours due to delayed recharge arrival, directly impacting potential conversions.</p>
<p><strong>Chaotic Multi-Channel Management</strong><br />
Many teams simultaneously work with 2-3 or more agent channels, with accounts scattered across different platforms and agents. Financial reconciliation requires verifying each account's recharge, spending, and refund records individually—manual operations are not only inefficient but also error-prone.</p>
<h2>What Systematic Dashboards Solve</h2>
<p>The essence of a dashboard is aggregating scattered information into a unified view. Yiyu's recently launched account management dashboard supports these core functions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Real-time account status sync</strong>: Opening progress, review results, and balance changes push automatically; teams no longer need repeated inquiries with agents.</li>
<li><strong>Automated recharge alerts</strong>: Notifications trigger when balance falls below set thresholds, supporting batch configuration across multiple accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Structured financial reconciliation</strong>: Recharge records, spending details, and refund amounts auto-aggregate by account and period, with exported reports directly usable for finance review.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-agent comparison</strong>: Display different agents' opening efficiency, account quality, and recharge speed within the same dashboard, assisting channel evaluation.</li>
</ul>
<p>After connecting to the dashboard, one content app team reduced finance reconciliation time from 8 hours to under 2 hours weekly, with account status inquiry volume dropping by approximately 60%.</p>
<h2>This Week's Recommendations</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inventory existing account data first</strong>: Categorize active accounts by agent, platform, and balance status to identify management blind spots.</li>
<li><strong>Start from the highest-frequency pain point</strong>: If recharge alerts are your biggest headache, prioritize configuring balance reminders; if reconciliation takes the most time, connect financial data first.</li>
<li><strong>Set dashboard update frequency</strong>: Core data should sync daily; non-core data weekly updates suffice, avoiding information overload.</li>
</ol>
<p>Proxy account opening services are evolving from "labor-intensive" to "system-dependent." Dashboards are not optional features but basic infrastructure for multi-account operations teams. These problems from this week could be solved by a dashboard next week.</p>
